MONO/LEMMA M2-c119 L09 Yes. Hypotheses: f is bounded on [a, b]. For any two partitions P and Q of [a, b], L(f, P) <= U(f, Q). The proof passes to the common refinement of P and Q and applies Lemma 1.1.
MONO/LEMMA M2-c121 L09 Yes. Hypotheses: f is monotone on [a, b]. Then f is integrable, and for every partition P with mesh delta, 0 <= U(f, P) - L(f, P) <= delta |f(b) - f(a)|. The proof takes n equal parts with n > (b - a)|f(b) - f(a)|/epsilon.
MONO/LEMMA M2-c122 L09 Hypotheses: f is monotone on [a, b], P has mesh delta, and R is any Riemann sum of f for P. Then |R - integral of f| <= delta |f(b) - f(a)|.
MONO/LEMMA M2-c132 L09 f is nondecreasing on [a, b] when s <= t implies f(s) <= f(t), nonincreasing when s <= t implies f(s) >= f(t), and monotone when it is one of these two. A monotone function on [a, b] is bounded, because min(f(a), f(b)) <= f(x) <= max(f(a), f(b)) for every x.
MONO/LEMMA M2-c163 L11 Not as stated. Theorem 2.1 lists the integrability of u'v and uv' on [a, b] as a separate hypothesis, which this lesson does not derive from the differentiability of u and v. The worked examples check it: polynomials on [0, b] with b > 0 (Corollary 1.2) and monotone functions (Lesson 9, Theorem 2.2)
MONO/LEMMA M2-c179 L12 The series sum over k >= 1 of k^(-p) converges for every integer p >= 2. Hypotheses: Assumption 2.1 of Lesson 6 (through Theorem 2.2) and Assumption 2.2 (the monotone convergence property: a nondecreasing sequence bounded above has a finite limit). The partial sums are nondecreasing and bounded by 1
